        definition:
          'A network is linear when its response obeys homogeneity — scaling every source by k scales every response by k — and additivity, meaning the response to two sources applied together equals the sum of the responses to each applied alone. Resistors, inductors, capacitors and all four dependent sources satisfy both; diodes, saturating magnetic cores and switches do not. Every theorem in this module is a direct consequence of these two properties, so each fails exactly where linearity fails. Proportionality also gives a useful technique in its own right: assume a convenient output, work backwards to the source it would need, then scale the whole solution.',
        takeaway: 'Homogeneity plus additivity is the licence for the entire module; lose either and every theorem here goes with it.',
