        terms: ['Norton equivalent', 'short-circuit current', 'parallel resistance', 'dual of Thevenin', 'conversion'],
        definition:
          'Any linear two-terminal network can equally be replaced by a current source In in parallel with a resistance Rn, where In is the short-circuit current at the terminals and Rn equals the Thevenin resistance. Norton is the exact dual of Thevenin, and the two equivalents convert into each other by Vth = In*Rth with the resistance unchanged, so finding either gives both. Norton is the more convenient form when the network feeds parallel branches or when currents rather than voltages are the quantities of interest, which is why it dominates in transistor modelling.',
        takeaway: 'Norton is Thevenin with every word swapped; find one and you have the other for free.',
        visual: 'norton-thevenin-duality',
        visualSpec:
          'Two equivalent circuits side by side inside dashed boxes: the left holds Vth in series with Rth, the right holds In in parallel with Rn, both presenting the same terminal pair. A central conversion panel lists Vth = In*Rth, In = Vth/Rth and Rn = Rth, with a double-headed arrow. Beneath each circuit a defining measurement is illustrated: the left with terminals open and a voltmeter reading Vth, the right with terminals shorted and an ammeter reading In. A small note reads "same two numbers, two arrangements". On reveal: both circuits draw inside their dashed boxes simultaneously, then the conversion panel fills line by line with the double arrow appearing last, then the open-circuit voltmeter attaches to the left and reads, then the short-circuit ammeter attaches to the right and reads, then the note lands.',
