        terms: ['matched load', 'maximum power', 'efficiency', 'source resistance', 'design criterion'],
        definition:
          'A source of Thevenin voltage Vth and resistance Rth delivers maximum power to a load when RL equals Rth, at which point the delivered power is Vth squared over 4Rth. The result follows from differentiating the load power with respect to RL and setting the derivative to zero. Crucially, the efficiency at that operating point is exactly 50 percent, because the source resistance dissipates as much as the load does. Maximum power transfer is therefore the right criterion for signal circuits, where the available power is tiny and must not be wasted, and the wrong one for power distribution, where throwing away half the energy would be absurd.',
        takeaway: 'Match for maximum power and you get 50 percent efficiency — correct for a receiver, indefensible for a substation.',
        visual: 'max-power-curve-and-efficiency',
        visualSpec:
          'A plot with load resistance on the horizontal axis and two curves: load power rising to a clear maximum at RL = Rth then falling, tinted green, and efficiency rising monotonically towards 100 percent, tinted blue. A vertical dashed line at RL = Rth marks the power peak, where a marker on the efficiency curve is labelled "50 percent". Two application cards flank the plot: "radio receiver: match, power is scarce" tied to the peak, and "power distribution: RL >> Rth, efficiency is everything" tied to the far right of the efficiency curve. On reveal: the axes draw, then the green power curve sweeps up and over its peak, then the blue efficiency curve sweeps up towards its asymptote, then the dashed line drops at the peak and the 50 percent marker lands on the efficiency curve, then the two application cards slide in from either side pointing at their operating regions.',
