        terms: ['tank circuit', 'parallel resonance', 'admittance', 'impedance maximum', 'current magnification'],
        definition:
          'An ideal parallel R-L-C circuit is the exact dual of the series case, so every result transfers with voltage and current exchanged. Resonance occurs when the susceptances cancel, again at w0 = 1/sqrt(LC), but now the admittance is minimum and the impedance is maximum and equal to R. The supply current falls to a minimum while the currents circulating in the inductor and capacitor individually reach Q times the supply current, equal and opposite so they cancel at the terminals. The circulating current is the dual of the voltage magnification seen in the series case.',
        takeaway: 'Parallel resonance is series resonance with voltage and current swapped: impedance peaks, supply current dips, and the branch currents circulate at Q times the input.',
        visual: 'parallel-resonance-duality',
        visualSpec:
          'A two-column duality table with the series case on the left and the parallel case on the right, rows for: condition, impedance at resonance, supply quantity, magnified quantity, Q formula. Each right-hand entry is the dual of its left-hand partner, with matched colour coding. Below the table, a circuit diagram of the ideal tank shows a circulating current arrow looping round the L and C branches drawn bold, with a thin supply current arrow entering from the source, captioned "large circulating, small supply". On reveal: the table header draws, then each row fills left then right in turn with the colour pairs matching, then the tank circuit draws beneath, then the thin supply arrow animates in and the bold circulating loop arrow sweeps repeatedly around the L-C branches.',
        algo: [
          'Write the total admittance as Y = 1/R + j(wC - 1/(wL))',
          'Set the susceptances equal: wC = 1/(wL)',
          'Solve for w0 = 1/sqrt(LC), the same expression as the series case',
          'Note the impedance is maximum and equal to R at that frequency',
          'Compute the branch currents as Q times the supply current',
        ],
        complexity: { best: 'impedance rises to R, its maximum, and the supply current falls to V/R', avg: 'all series results transfer by duality', worst: 'a real inductor has series resistance, which breaks the exact duality', note: 'That breakage is treated in the next unit and is the main practical departure from the textbook ideal.' },
        dryRun: {
          input: 'Ideal parallel circuit, R = 10 kohm, L = 0.1 H, C = 10 microfarad, supply 100 V.',
          steps: [
            'w0 = 1/sqrt(0.1 x 10e-6) = 1000 rad/s, as in the series case',
            'Supply current at resonance: 100/10000 = 10 mA',
            'Capacitor branch current: V*wC = 100 x 1000 x 10e-6 = 1 A',
            'Inductor branch current: V/(wL) = 100/100 = 1 A, in antiphase',
          ],
          result: '1 A circulating in the tank while only 10 mA is drawn from the supply — a current magnification of 100, which is Q. The two branch currents cancel exactly at the terminals.',
        },
        code: { lang: 'math', src: 'Y = 1/R + j( wC - 1/(wL) )\n\nResonance:  wC = 1/(wL)  =>  w0 = 1/sqrt(L*C)\n\nAt w0:\n  Y = 1/R minimum,  Z = R maximum\n  supply current = V/R, minimum\n  IL = IC = Q * I_supply,  in antiphase\n\nQ = R/(w0*L) = w0*C*R = R*sqrt(C/L)' },
        mistake: 'Students carry the series Q formula w0L/R into the parallel case. The parallel Q is R/(w0L), the reciprocal form, because R now appears in parallel rather than in series; using the wrong one inverts the answer.',
        book: { chapter: 12, pages: '287-288' },
        bloom: 'L4',
        co: 'CO2',
