        slides: [29, 30, 31],
        terms: ['initial condition', 'final condition', 'steady state', 'switching instant', 'well-posed problem'],
        definition:
          'A circuit containing inductors or capacitors is described by differential equations, and a differential equation has infinitely many solutions until initial conditions select one. The initial conditions are the inductor currents and capacitor voltages at the instant t = 0+, immediately after switching; the final conditions are the same quantities as t tends to infinity, when all derivatives have died and the circuit has reached steady state. Knowing both lets the complete response be written down without solving the equation at all, because the solution is a decay from the initial value to the final one.',
        takeaway: 'Start value, end value and time constant are enough to write the whole response — the differential equation need never be solved.',
