        definition:
          'The one-sided Laplace transform of a function f(t) defined for t >= 0 is F(s) = the integral from zero to infinity of f(t) times exp(-st) dt, where s is complex. The exponential kernel decays for sufficiently large real part of s, which is what allows the integral to converge even for functions that grow, and the set of s for which it converges is the region of convergence. Circuit analysis uses the one-sided form because circuits are switched at a definite instant and nothing before t = 0 is of interest — which is also what lets the initial conditions enter the transform of a derivative.',
        takeaway: 'Multiply by a decaying exponential and integrate: the decay is what makes even growing functions transformable.',
