        definition:
          'A three-phase system is unbalanced when the three phase impedances differ, or less commonly when the source magnitudes or angles do. In practice the load side is almost always the culprit: single-phase loads such as lighting and domestic supplies are connected between one phase and neutral, and although installers distribute them across phases the distribution is never exact and varies through the day. Faults produce severe unbalance suddenly. The consequence for analysis is that the per-phase shortcut is void, the three phase currents differ in magnitude and are no longer 120 degrees apart, and their sum is no longer zero.',
