        definition:
          'When a neutral conductor of negligible impedance joins the load star point to the source star point, the two star points are held at the same potential and each phase sees its full phase voltage regardless of what the other phases are doing. The three phases therefore decouple completely: each phase current is simply its phase voltage divided by its own impedance, computed independently. The neutral conductor carries the phasor sum of the three currents, which is zero only in the balanced case. This is the easy case of unbalanced analysis, and it is why distribution systems supplying single-phase loads always include a neutral.',
