        definition:
          'Blondel theorem states that the power in an n-wire system can be measured with n-1 wattmeters, so a three-wire three-phase system needs only two regardless of balance. Each wattmeter takes its current from one line and its voltage between that line and the third, and the algebraic sum of the two readings is the total real power for any load. For a balanced load the two readings also give the power factor angle through their ratio, and one reading goes negative when the power factor falls below 0.5, which is a real measurement rather than an instrument fault.',
